1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:10,350
Okay, welcome back. So I hope that you were able to sort of solidify the mediators and cardinal signs of acute inflammation in your mind,

2
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:18,990
and this discussion that we're now going to enter is sort of the second half of the discussion of acute inflammation.

3
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:25,890
So the next real important factor in acute inflammation is going to be neutrophil arrival and function.

4
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:34,850
Essentially what has occurred in the first half of inflammation is that we've really generated increased vascular permeability.

5
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:43,390
We've generated edema, swelling, edema leading to swelling and warmth due to increased blood within the actual tissue,

6
00:00:43,790 --> 00:00:48,850
and now the next goal of acute inflammation would be to allow for neutrophil arrival and function.

7
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:56,410
It might be worth just taking a step back here and reminding ourselves from basic pathology that when you think about acute inflammation,

8
00:00:56,570 --> 00:00:59,850
if this is a timeline, it's really divided into three phases.

9
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:05,370
So there's sort of the fluid phase, and then there's the neutrophil phase, and then there's the macrophage phase.

10
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:09,090
And so this first phase sort of peaks here, and this is the fluid phase.

11
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:13,490
And the goal of the initial phase of acute inflammation is to really bring fluid and edema

12
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:18,390
and some of the important activating proteins like complement into the tissue space.

13
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:22,470
And then the next phase of acute inflammation is actually the neutrophil phase,

14
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:28,650
and it peaks at about 24 hours, and that's when most of the neutrophils have actually come into the tissue.

15
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,810
And then the third phase of acute inflammation is actually the macrophage phase,

16
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:37,290
and this macrophage phase essentially peaks at about two to three days.

17
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:43,550
So maybe I'm just sort of providing this as a global picture of acute inflammation.

18
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:47,430
So the way I think about acute inflammation, it has sort of three distinct phases.

19
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:51,770
So anyway, what we really want to focus on here is now the neutrophil phase,

20
00:01:51,850 --> 00:01:55,710
how the neutrophils arrive and what they actually do when they get into the tissue.

21
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:59,610
So let's move forward and talk about how the neutrophils actually get there.

22
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:03,810
Before I actually explain this in step-by-step detail,

23
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:10,090
it might be probably best to actually take a step back and give you a global picture of what's going to happen.

24
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:15,950
So let's clear the screen here and give you just a little bit of an overview.

25
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:20,190
Now normally when you have a blood vessel, so pretend this is a blood vessel,

26
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:27,250
one of the important dynamics of blood flow is that the heavy particles tend to sit in the center of the flow.

27
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:31,250
So as blood is flowing in this lamellar or linear manner,

28
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:34,830
you recall that blood flows in a very organized way,

29
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:38,370
the heavy particles will tend to sit at the center of the blood vessel.

30
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:45,410
And one of the very first things that's going to happen or the early things that's going to happen is vasodilation.

31
00:02:45,710 --> 00:02:49,270
And as we get vasodilation, this blood vessel now becomes much wider.

32
00:02:49,270 --> 00:02:56,970
And one of the results of vasodilation will be that the particles that were sort of running in the center of the flow,

33
00:02:57,490 --> 00:03:02,590
by the fact that they'll enter this area of vasodilation where the blood flow becomes a little more turbulent,

34
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:06,510
the heavy particles will be allowed to come to the edge of the blood flow.

35
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:11,270
And when the heavy particles come to the edge of the blood flow, that's actually called margination.

36
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:17,690
And so margination is the very initial or first step of neutrophil arrival.

37
00:03:18,090 --> 00:03:23,410
So let's go back and now look at step one of margination with that in the back of our mind.

38
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:25,810
So what do I say about step one?

39
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:27,630
I say step one margination.

40
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:30,850
Vasodilation slows the blood flow.

41
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:31,390
Where?

42
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:33,710
Post-capillary venule.

43
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:35,050
Again, very high yield.

44
00:03:35,170 --> 00:03:39,490
That the neutrophils are actually going to come in in the same place that the fluid actually comes from,

45
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:41,250
which is the post-capillary venule.

46
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:44,030
Now, because we get that vasodilation,

47
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:48,010
the cells are going to marginate from the center of the flow to the periphery.

48
00:03:48,090 --> 00:03:51,370
And that's basically what I was trying to say here in your text.

49
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:54,210
The next step is actually called rolling.

50
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:57,450
Now what's going to happen here is that remember that we've got this blood vessel,

51
00:03:57,630 --> 00:03:59,730
and then the blood vessel actually vasodilated,

52
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:04,730
and with the vasodilation we had margination so that the cells were out here at the periphery.

53
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:08,110
Well, on the next step in rolling, what's going to happen is that these cells

54
00:04:08,110 --> 00:04:11,870
that have now marginated to the periphery need to be slowed down.

55
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:15,250
And the way that the cells are going to slow down is that they're going to hit these little,

56
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:16,910
what I call, speed bumps.

57
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:20,270
In fact, the technical name for them is selectins.

58
00:04:20,690 --> 00:04:25,070
So the selectin speed bumps will be upregulated on the endothelial cells,

59
00:04:25,190 --> 00:04:28,830
i.e. the endothelial cells, which are the cells that line the blood vessel,

60
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:31,510
will begin to express selectins,

61
00:04:31,510 --> 00:04:38,810
and the selectins will actually cause the neutrophils to slow down along that blood vessel periphery.

62
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:40,230
And that's called rolling.

63
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:47,250
So these neutrophils will begin to roll slowly along these selectin speed bumps.

64
00:04:47,810 --> 00:04:50,550
And so that's what the next step is in acute inflammation.

65
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:51,350
It's called rolling.

66
00:04:51,490 --> 00:04:53,930
Now let's read a little bit about this in detail.

67
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:55,450
So what do I say in your text?

68
00:04:55,450 --> 00:05:00,230
I say that selectin speed bumps are upregulated on the endothelial cells.

69
00:05:00,410 --> 00:05:03,750
And I've made that very clear already by the image that I just drew.

70
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:10,290
Now what's important to know for examinations beyond the fact that it's selectins that are going to be upregulated

71
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:12,830
is that there's actually two subtypes of selectins.

72
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:15,630
The first selectin is called P-selectin,

73
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:18,870
and the second selectin is called E-selectin.

74
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:23,630
These are the two selectins, and they'll be upregulated on the endothelial cell.

75
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:29,710
It's important for examinations to know that P-selectins come from the Weibol-Pilotti bodies.

76
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:32,850
Again, P-selectin comes from the Weibol-Pilotti body.

77
00:05:33,090 --> 00:05:37,270
Let's go back to normal histology and remind ourselves that within the endothelial cells,

78
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:39,830
particularly seen on electron microscopy,

79
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:43,630
are small little bodies, preformed bodies, called Weibol-Pilotti bodies,

80
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:48,390
which have some proteins that are necessary for function of the endothelial cells.

81
00:05:48,390 --> 00:05:53,410
To me, this is a very easy thing to remember

82
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:59,250
because I predominantly remember that the Weibol-Pilotti bodies contain two important proteins

83
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:02,770
and that the W gives away one and that the P gives away the other.

84
00:06:03,450 --> 00:06:04,810
So that's my little mnemonic.

85
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:07,890
So the P, for me, reminds me of P-selectin.

86
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:10,770
So the Weibol-Pilotti bodies contain P-selectin.

87
00:06:10,770 --> 00:06:15,510
And can you remember what the other important protein is that the W might sort of hint at?

88
00:06:16,830 --> 00:06:18,310
Von Willebrand's factor.

89
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:20,810
So the W for Willebrand.

90
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:24,410
So for me, the two key proteins within the Weibol-Pilotti body,

91
00:06:24,730 --> 00:06:27,250
the W stands for Von Willebrand's factor,

92
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:30,010
and the P reminds me of P-selectin.

93
00:06:30,230 --> 00:06:31,470
So that's how I remember that.

94
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:33,830
Anyway, P-selectin gets upregulated,

95
00:06:34,010 --> 00:06:36,510
and this upregulation is mediated by histamine.

96
00:06:36,510 --> 00:06:43,430
At the same time, e-selectin production is induced by TNF and IL-1.

97
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:45,470
So I'm sorry, and it's unfortunate,

98
00:06:45,690 --> 00:06:49,730
but you do have to remember that e-selectin is induced by TNF and IL-1,

99
00:06:49,810 --> 00:06:51,890
and that's sort of one of those things that we have to memorize.

100
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:55,750
And I hate memorization, but we do have to memorize that.

101
00:06:56,230 --> 00:07:01,250
So this is the mechanism by which selectins get upregulated on the endothelial cells.

102
00:07:01,790 --> 00:07:05,430
Now, beyond the upregulation of selectin on endothelial cells,

103
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:10,290
it's important to note that the selectins are actually going to bind a molecule on the leukocytes,

104
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,490
on the neutrophils, which is Sial-Lewis-X.

105
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:14,910
We do have to know that molecule.

106
00:07:15,510 --> 00:07:20,130
So that it's Sial-Lewis-X that actually is going to specifically bump into the selectins

107
00:07:20,130 --> 00:07:23,070
and allow for the rolling of the neutrophils.

108
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:28,790
Now, this interaction results in rolling of the leukocytes along the vessel wall,

109
00:07:28,930 --> 00:07:33,450
as I've already explained, which sort of then slows down the neutrophil.

110
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:37,370
And by the way, you might ask, well, why am I using the term leukocytes here?

111
00:07:38,310 --> 00:07:45,010
Leukocytes refers to white blood cells, and the neutrophils use this mechanism,

112
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:48,750
and you're going to hear eventually that the macrophages use this mechanism as well.

113
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:53,490
So I've left the term general, although we're actually specifically discussing neutrophils here.

114
00:07:53,630 --> 00:07:53,830
Okay?

115
00:07:54,110 --> 00:07:55,590
So I hope that that doesn't confuse you.

116
00:07:55,630 --> 00:07:58,210
I just want your text to be as accurate as possible.

117
00:07:58,210 --> 00:08:00,470
And I'll make this point a little later as well.

118
00:08:01,210 --> 00:08:03,790
Now, the next step is called adhesion.

119
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:06,550
And the idea here, I'm going to blank out your screen briefly,

120
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:08,590
is that you've got this blood vessel.

121
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:11,210
We vasodilated the blood vessel, allowing for margination.

122
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:16,070
We upregulated the selectin P and E, selectin speed bumps,

123
00:08:16,390 --> 00:08:19,910
which then hit Sial-Lewis-X on the leukocytes,

124
00:08:20,230 --> 00:08:23,890
which caused the leukocytes to slow down as they roll along that blood vessel.

125
00:08:23,890 --> 00:08:30,290
Now, eventually what we're going to want to do is we're going to want to grab that leukocyte very tightly and stop it.

126
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:34,830
And the reason we're going to want to stop it dead in its tracks is because eventually we want to bring the leukocyte

127
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:39,370
to this side of the blood vessel so that it can fight the infection out here.

128
00:08:39,930 --> 00:08:42,870
And so the next step is actually called adhesion.

129
00:08:43,270 --> 00:08:44,030
So let's go back.

130
00:08:44,230 --> 00:08:49,630
And adhesion refers to the binding of the leukocyte to the vessel wall,

131
00:08:49,630 --> 00:08:53,590
and it actually occurs via an interaction between two proteins.

132
00:08:53,890 --> 00:08:58,650
So on the endothelium, on the cells that line the blood vessel wall, i.e. the endothelium,

133
00:08:59,130 --> 00:09:04,390
the key molecule that's going to be upregulated are cellular adhesion molecules.

134
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:09,110
And these are upregulated by TNF and IL-1.

135
00:09:09,390 --> 00:09:12,490
So we can see that TNF and IL-1 are actually playing a very important role

136
00:09:12,490 --> 00:09:17,190
in this initial phase of bringing in the neutrophil into the tissue.

137
00:09:17,750 --> 00:09:20,470
So cellular adhesion molecules, also called CAMs.

138
00:09:20,550 --> 00:09:22,990
And you can go into a little bit of detail here.

139
00:09:23,090 --> 00:09:25,070
There's an ICAM and there's a V-CAM.

140
00:09:25,410 --> 00:09:29,130
But I think it's sufficient for you to know that these are just cellular adhesion molecules.

141
00:09:29,130 --> 00:09:32,790
Now, these cellular adhesion molecules are going to bind integrins.

142
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:35,710
And integrins are present on the neutrophils or on the leukocytes.

143
00:09:36,390 --> 00:09:41,490
And the integrins are upregulated by C5A and LTB4.

144
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:45,670
But that's not hard to remember because do you remember what are our key four molecules

145
00:09:45,670 --> 00:09:46,850
that bring in the neutrophils?

146
00:09:47,410 --> 00:09:47,870
Very good.

147
00:09:48,250 --> 00:09:53,050
C5A, LTB4, IL-8, and bacterial products.

148
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:59,170
So it's not hard to remember that the molecules that help to activate and draw in the neutrophils

149
00:09:59,170 --> 00:10:02,450
are going to be the same molecules that are going to activate the neutrophils

150
00:10:02,450 --> 00:10:05,830
at this particular phase of adhesion.

151
00:10:06,350 --> 00:10:12,130
And it's this firm interaction between cellular adhesion molecules and C5A and integrins

152
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:14,690
that then results in a firm adhesion to the vessel wall.

153
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:17,250
I'm going to draw this for you because, again, it's very high yield.

154
00:10:17,710 --> 00:10:19,890
So let me erase this and give you a blank screen.

155
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:21,790
This is the vessel wall.

156
00:10:21,790 --> 00:10:27,010
All right, and the vessel wall is lined by endothelium, as you're well aware.

157
00:10:27,490 --> 00:10:33,710
And this endothelium is going to express something called a cellular adhesion molecule.

158
00:10:34,010 --> 00:10:36,810
So let's make this the cellular adhesion molecule.

159
00:10:37,350 --> 00:10:40,870
And this is the leukocyte, the neutrophil that we're trying to draw in.

160
00:10:41,090 --> 00:10:44,650
And the neutrophil is going to express integrins.

161
00:10:45,550 --> 00:10:48,870
All right, and the integrins are going to bind the cellular adhesion molecule.

162
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:55,110
This is then going to result in a firm adhesion of the neutrophil to the blood vessel wall.

163
00:10:55,590 --> 00:11:01,710
Again, do you guys remember what upregulates the cellular adhesion molecule?

164
00:11:02,610 --> 00:11:03,150
Very good.

165
00:11:03,350 --> 00:11:04,870
TNF and IL-1.

166
00:11:05,170 --> 00:11:07,110
And what upregulates the integrin?

167
00:11:07,110 --> 00:11:10,750
C5A, LTB4.

168
00:11:10,890 --> 00:11:11,370
Very good.

169
00:11:11,690 --> 00:11:16,490
Again, this adhesion process is eventually going to then allow for us to bring the neutrophil

170
00:11:16,490 --> 00:11:21,530
across the blood vessel into the tissue space.

171
00:11:21,970 --> 00:11:25,230
But before we do that, there's an important disease that we want to discuss.

172
00:11:25,570 --> 00:11:27,190
And this disease is very high yield.

173
00:11:27,570 --> 00:11:29,450
It's called leukocyte adhesion deficiency.

174
00:11:29,450 --> 00:11:34,450
Now, the problem in leukocyte adhesion deficiency is that the patient has an autosomal recessive

175
00:11:34,450 --> 00:11:37,670
defect of integrin, one of the subunits of integrin.

176
00:11:37,930 --> 00:11:42,950
And you do need to know for examination that it is the CD18 subunit.

177
00:11:43,430 --> 00:11:48,690
When there is defect of integrin, as you just learned, integrin is necessary for the neutrophil

178
00:11:48,690 --> 00:11:53,010
to actually be able to bind the cellular adhesion molecules on the endothelium.

179
00:11:53,010 --> 00:11:59,430
If there is no integrin, the leukocytes will not be able to adhese to the endothelium and

180
00:11:59,430 --> 00:12:02,850
they will not be able to be drawn eventually into the tissue.

181
00:12:03,290 --> 00:12:05,110
So guess what the clinical findings are going to be?

182
00:12:05,950 --> 00:12:10,150
Well, one of the key clinical findings is delayed separation of the umbilical cord.

183
00:12:10,870 --> 00:12:15,710
Very, very early finding and very high yield for examinations.

184
00:12:16,170 --> 00:12:17,430
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves.

185
00:12:17,430 --> 00:12:24,350
When a baby is born, the umbilical cord, which normally had blood flowing through it, is sealed.

186
00:12:24,690 --> 00:12:28,470
It's clamped and itself, it seals once it's disconnected from the placenta.

187
00:12:28,990 --> 00:12:32,310
And the next thing that's going to happen to the umbilical cord is that the umbilical cord

188
00:12:32,310 --> 00:12:35,190
is going to die because there's no more blood going through it.

189
00:12:35,390 --> 00:12:36,730
There's no more blood feeding it.

190
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:38,510
So the tissue will undergo necrosis.

191
00:12:38,890 --> 00:12:41,910
And do you remember, what's the result of necrosis?

192
00:12:42,030 --> 00:12:43,750
What's the next step after necrosis?

193
00:12:44,250 --> 00:12:45,390
Very good, acute inflammation.

194
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:49,210
Remember that acute inflammation comes in after necrosis

195
00:12:49,210 --> 00:12:52,170
and allows for the tissue to be destroyed so that it can be healed.

196
00:12:52,830 --> 00:12:55,190
Now, once the umbilical cord has been,

197
00:12:55,650 --> 00:12:58,750
the blood supply of the umbilical cord has been cut off, the tissue will die.

198
00:12:59,130 --> 00:13:02,010
And eventually, the umbilical cord will simply just fall off.

199
00:13:02,210 --> 00:13:03,670
And it will separate from the baby.

200
00:13:04,110 --> 00:13:07,370
And what helps it to fall off is the neutrophils come in

201
00:13:07,370 --> 00:13:08,870
and they actually destroy the tissue.

202
00:13:09,150 --> 00:13:11,990
And that then allows for separation of the umbilical cord.

203
00:13:11,990 --> 00:13:16,390
However, if the neutrophils can't come in,

204
00:13:16,650 --> 00:13:24,350
that separation of that necrotic umbilical cord from the baby's abdomen or skin will actually be delayed.

205
00:13:24,810 --> 00:13:26,490
And so that's the key clinical feature.

206
00:13:26,590 --> 00:13:29,930
And it ties into so many of the principles that we've already been speaking about,

207
00:13:30,370 --> 00:13:34,770
i.e. the whole notion that necrosis is followed by acute inflammation,

208
00:13:34,770 --> 00:13:40,630
and then the whole notion that we need this sequence of margination, rolling, and adhesion

209
00:13:40,630 --> 00:13:42,870
to eventually bring neutrophils into the tissue.

210
00:13:43,070 --> 00:13:48,690
And because we're lacking the CD18 subunit of integrin in this particular disease,

211
00:13:49,050 --> 00:13:52,790
the neutrophils actually can't arrive because they can't adhese properly.

212
00:13:53,210 --> 00:13:54,550
So that's one important finding.

213
00:13:54,770 --> 00:13:56,110
And that, again, is kind of high yield.

214
00:13:56,110 --> 00:14:01,910
Another important finding that we would see in leukocyte adhesion deficiency

215
00:14:01,910 --> 00:14:03,850
is increased circulating neutrophils.

216
00:14:03,970 --> 00:14:06,750
And this is also a very important sort of high yield topic.

217
00:14:07,250 --> 00:14:10,610
Let me go back and remind you that when you think about your neutrophils,

218
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:13,710
you can divide your neutrophils into sort of two groups.

219
00:14:14,110 --> 00:14:16,610
There are those neutrophils that are floating around in the blood,

220
00:14:16,730 --> 00:14:18,910
and they represent about 50% of your neutrophils.

221
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:22,730
But the other half of your neutrophils are actually sort of hanging out

222
00:14:22,730 --> 00:14:24,770
within the blood vessels of the lung.

223
00:14:26,110 --> 00:14:43,590
And they sort of sit there, and they are hanging there so that if they're needed,

224
00:14:43,990 --> 00:14:46,430
they can immediately come out into the blood.

225
00:14:46,650 --> 00:14:49,390
So it's sort of their present waiting to be activated,

226
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:52,070
waiting to be called in to do something.

227
00:14:52,070 --> 00:14:56,270
And the way by which these neutrophils, they're called the marginated pool.

228
00:14:56,610 --> 00:15:01,550
And the way by which the marginated pool is able to hang onto the blood vessel in the lung

229
00:15:01,550 --> 00:15:03,910
is using adhesion.

230
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:08,870
So if adhesion is defective, then the marginated pool in the lung

231
00:15:08,870 --> 00:15:11,950
will no longer be able to sort of hang onto the blood vessel in the lung

232
00:15:11,950 --> 00:15:13,570
and wait for battle.

233
00:15:14,030 --> 00:15:16,350
And instead, that will be released into the blood,

234
00:15:16,350 --> 00:15:20,670
and that's going to give us an increased circulating neutrophil count.

235
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:23,930
And so that's why these patients have increased circulating neutrophils.

236
00:15:24,450 --> 00:15:29,910
And then the third finding present in these patients is that they get recurrent bacterial infections.

237
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:32,930
But what's important is that they lack pus.

238
00:15:33,370 --> 00:15:35,590
They get infections, but they don't form pus.

239
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:36,970
Let's go back and remind ourselves.

240
00:15:37,070 --> 00:15:37,650
What is pus?

241
00:15:38,090 --> 00:15:41,070
Pus is dead neutrophils sitting in fluid.

242
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:43,010
That's basically what pus is.

243
00:15:43,130 --> 00:15:45,110
Pus is dead neutrophils sitting in fluid.

244
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:49,730
And so if the patients can't get neutrophils into the tissue,

245
00:15:49,910 --> 00:15:51,590
they're not going to be able to form pus.

246
00:15:51,830 --> 00:15:56,250
So they'll get recurrent bacterial infections that particularly lack pus formation.

247
00:15:56,690 --> 00:15:58,190
And that's important as well.

248
00:15:58,810 --> 00:16:05,090
So these are the three key clinical features when a patient is lacking integrins

249
00:16:05,090 --> 00:16:09,370
and therefore can't produce proper leukocyte adhesion.

250
00:16:09,370 --> 00:16:15,090
So let's go on to the next step in the arrival of neutrophils,

251
00:16:15,210 --> 00:16:18,190
and that is transmigration in chemotaxis.

252
00:16:18,690 --> 00:16:21,650
Now the idea here, again, I'm going to create a white screen for a moment.

253
00:16:21,850 --> 00:16:23,730
The idea here, if this is the blood vessel,

254
00:16:24,130 --> 00:16:27,290
and the blood vessel is obviously lined by endothelium,

255
00:16:27,550 --> 00:16:32,650
and we've got this neutrophil, and the neutrophil is adhesed here and is stuck,

256
00:16:32,650 --> 00:16:36,930
and the next step is going to be that we're going to bring this neutrophil across the blood vessel.

257
00:16:37,030 --> 00:16:41,730
That's called transmigration, to go transmigration, across, to migrate across.

258
00:16:42,250 --> 00:16:47,670
And then the neutrophil, once it gets here, is going to be drawn into the area of battle over here.

259
00:16:47,950 --> 00:16:51,470
And the movement of the neutrophil to this area, that's called chemotaxis.

260
00:16:51,590 --> 00:16:54,630
And it's called chemotaxis because the neutrophil can't see,

261
00:16:54,630 --> 00:16:58,770
but it can sense chemicals, chemo, chemical, taxis, chemical movement.

262
00:16:59,310 --> 00:17:03,110
And it senses chemicals, and the chemicals sort of call the neutrophil to the area of battle.

263
00:17:03,510 --> 00:17:06,990
So let's go back to our slides and now sort of read together.

264
00:17:07,310 --> 00:17:11,210
So the leukocytes transmigrate across the endothelium of what?

265
00:17:11,730 --> 00:17:12,530
Postcapillary venules.

266
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:13,710
I can't hammer that home enough.

267
00:17:13,810 --> 00:17:15,170
That shows up on exams often.

268
00:17:15,310 --> 00:17:18,150
It's the postcapillary venules where the neutrophils are doing all of this.

269
00:17:18,670 --> 00:17:22,710
They then move towards chemical attractants, which is called chemotaxis,

270
00:17:22,710 --> 00:17:24,770
and guess what the chemical attractants are?

271
00:17:25,090 --> 00:17:27,770
The four key products that call in neutrophils.

272
00:17:27,870 --> 00:17:29,110
And you're already familiar with this.

273
00:17:29,690 --> 00:17:33,870
C5A, LTB4, IL-8, and bacterial products.

274
00:17:34,930 --> 00:17:39,470
Once the neutrophils get to the area of battle, the way by which they kill is phagocytosis.

275
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:43,350
And that is step five in this sequence, phagocytosis.

276
00:17:44,070 --> 00:17:47,650
Phagocytosis is the consumption of pathogens or necrotic tissue.

277
00:17:47,650 --> 00:17:49,810
Again, neutrophils are drawn in by infection,

278
00:17:49,810 --> 00:17:52,830
or they can be drawn in by tissue necrosis.

279
00:17:53,410 --> 00:17:56,190
And phagocytosis occurs, for the most part, occurs blindly.

280
00:17:56,350 --> 00:17:58,070
The neutrophil goes in, it's activated,

281
00:17:58,230 --> 00:18:00,230
and just begins to eat and consume and destroy.

282
00:18:00,750 --> 00:18:05,210
But one of the ways by which we can enhance phagocytosis is by opsonins.

283
00:18:05,650 --> 00:18:08,230
Opsonins are molecules that the neutrophils can recognize.

284
00:18:08,590 --> 00:18:10,230
And when the neutrophils see these molecules,

285
00:18:10,350 --> 00:18:13,090
they know that, aha, this is the thing that I'm supposed to eat.

286
00:18:13,090 --> 00:18:18,810
So when it sees IgG, IgG is an opsonin that allows the neutrophil,

287
00:18:19,010 --> 00:18:23,390
that the neutrophil can recognize and then eat whatever is bound to the IgG.

288
00:18:23,830 --> 00:18:27,430
And similarly, another important opsonin is C3B.

289
00:18:27,890 --> 00:18:29,870
Now, I would like to stop here for a moment,

290
00:18:30,070 --> 00:18:32,030
and we're on page 13 of your text.

291
00:18:32,430 --> 00:18:36,810
Under neutrophil arrival and function, letter E, step five, phagocytosis.

292
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:42,470
Under point one, I specifically say consumption of pathogens or necrotic tissue,

293
00:18:42,670 --> 00:18:45,610
semicolon, phagocytosis is enhanced by opsonins.

294
00:18:46,070 --> 00:18:52,230
And notice I put in parentheses there, IgG and C3, it should say B in your text.

295
00:18:52,570 --> 00:18:53,570
There's a typo there.

296
00:18:53,730 --> 00:18:57,810
Please correct that on page 13 and make that say C3B.

297
00:18:58,950 --> 00:19:02,950
So anyway, that's step five, and that is phagocytosis.

298
00:19:03,390 --> 00:19:04,990
So let's keep going.

299
00:19:04,990 --> 00:19:12,630
The way by which phagocytosis occurs is that pseudopods from the leukocytes extend to form phagosomes.

300
00:19:12,870 --> 00:19:13,850
And so here's the idea.

301
00:19:14,210 --> 00:19:17,930
If this was the neutrophil, all right, what will the neutrophil do?

302
00:19:18,050 --> 00:19:21,970
It will sort of draw out these big arms, which are like pseudopods.

303
00:19:22,350 --> 00:19:24,730
And let's say that this is the thing that it wants to consume.

304
00:19:24,950 --> 00:19:28,110
So it will then wrap its arms around this molecule.

305
00:19:28,110 --> 00:19:38,850
And eventually it will result in the neutrophil containing a little vesicle, which actually contains the product that needs to be destroyed.

306
00:19:39,690 --> 00:19:46,210
And so the pseudopods, which are these fingers, will form, from the leukocytes, extend to form phagosomes.

307
00:19:46,210 --> 00:19:51,410
And this is the phagosome here, this circular space that gets internalized within the neutrophil.

308
00:19:51,850 --> 00:19:58,530
So the phagosome is internalized, and eventually the phagosome will move, and it will merge with something called the lysosome.

309
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:08,050
And we are well aware from cellular biology that the lysosome contains the highly degradative enzymes necessary to destroy things.

310
00:20:08,050 --> 00:20:15,550
And so this phagosome will eventually merge with the lysosome, and when it merges with the lysosome, it is called a phagolysosome.

311
00:20:16,150 --> 00:20:17,950
So a phagolysosome is formed.

312
00:20:18,670 --> 00:20:31,290
One of the important defects that can impair this production of the phagolysosome is called Chediakigashi syndrome, or involves Chediakigashi syndrome, high yield for examination.

313
00:20:31,610 --> 00:20:34,210
So let's take a moment and divert our discussion.

314
00:20:34,630 --> 00:20:35,930
What is Chediakigashi syndrome?

315
00:20:35,930 --> 00:20:41,230
The way I remember Chediakigashi syndrome is that basically it's a protein trafficking defect.

316
00:20:41,650 --> 00:20:47,210
It's an autosomal recessive disease, and perhaps it might be best to sort of just do this with a little picture.

317
00:20:47,350 --> 00:20:51,110
So let me draw you a picture and just remind you that let's say this is the neutrophil.

318
00:20:51,450 --> 00:20:58,030
As I mentioned, it's going to produce this phagosome where it eats some sort of particle and brings it inside the neutrophil.

319
00:20:58,030 --> 00:21:10,690
And the next step is going to be to move that phagosome and merge it with the lysosome to form the phagolysosome because it's within the lysosome that the key enzymes for destruction are present.

320
00:21:11,450 --> 00:21:20,730
Now, any time the cell wants to move something, and notice that we're trying to move this big phagosome, it needs to do it across, quote-unquote, a railroad system.

321
00:21:20,730 --> 00:21:26,410
So that there's sort of got to be these tracks that allow the phagosome to be guided to the lysosome.

322
00:21:26,970 --> 00:21:31,290
So this railroad system is what's defective in Chediakigashi syndrome.

323
00:21:31,570 --> 00:21:38,010
The railroad system is actually made up of microtubules, and Chediakigashi syndrome can be thought of as a microtubule defect.

324
00:21:38,010 --> 00:21:49,790
So if the microtubules are out, the railroad system is out, and if the railroad system is out, then we can't traffic this phagosome to merge it with the lysosome.

325
00:21:50,250 --> 00:21:54,010
And so, therefore, all of the clinical symptoms are going to be related to that.

326
00:21:54,270 --> 00:21:56,690
So let's go back now and review the clinical symptoms.

327
00:21:57,290 --> 00:21:59,670
The clinical features are going to include the following.

328
00:22:00,110 --> 00:22:02,790
Number one, increased risk of pyogenic infections.

329
00:22:03,270 --> 00:22:05,410
Why would there be an increased risk of pyogenic infections?

330
00:22:05,410 --> 00:22:14,930
Well, I've just shown you in an image that the phagosome is created, but it can't merge with the lysosome because the railroad is sort of out.

331
00:22:15,030 --> 00:22:15,850
The tracks are out.

332
00:22:16,370 --> 00:22:23,190
And so, therefore, patients are going to have an increased risk of infection because they can't destroy the organisms that the neutrophils consume.

333
00:22:23,830 --> 00:22:25,710
Another problem is going to be neutropenia.

334
00:22:25,810 --> 00:22:30,030
Now, the idea here is that the neutrophils within the bone marrow, when they're dividing,

335
00:22:30,030 --> 00:22:35,350
remember that if you've got one cell and you actually want to divide it into two cells,

336
00:22:35,470 --> 00:22:41,250
what you have to actually do is sort of separate the cells and then move some cellular components this way,

337
00:22:41,370 --> 00:22:45,650
move some cellular components that way, move some DNA this way, move some DNA that way,

338
00:22:45,710 --> 00:22:48,270
so that you can eventually produce two separate cells.

339
00:22:48,710 --> 00:22:53,390
However, if you're not able to move things around properly, you're not going to be able to divide properly,

340
00:22:53,390 --> 00:22:59,650
and therefore, because the neutrophils can't divide properly, there's a defect in generation within the bone marrow of neutrophils,

341
00:22:59,950 --> 00:23:01,850
and so these patients get neutropenia.

342
00:23:02,450 --> 00:23:06,470
Another problem that's going to occur is that the patients get giant granules in their leukocytes.

343
00:23:06,570 --> 00:23:07,270
Now, let's go back.

344
00:23:07,730 --> 00:23:14,950
The granules within the leukocytes and the neutrophils particularly are actually produced from the Golgi apparatus.

345
00:23:14,950 --> 00:23:19,330
So, if there's a Golgi apparatus here, and these granules are being produced,

346
00:23:19,770 --> 00:23:23,770
these granules, after they're produced, will actually go along the railroad system,

347
00:23:23,850 --> 00:23:29,070
and this granule may go here, and another granule may go here, and another granule may go here,

348
00:23:29,390 --> 00:23:32,510
and that's how the granules get distributed across the neutrophils,

349
00:23:32,610 --> 00:23:38,470
and when you look at a slide, you can sometimes even see the granulations present within the cytoplasm of the neutrophil.

350
00:23:38,470 --> 00:23:46,890
However, if there's a defect, and none of these granules can be sent off to the periphery and distributed across the cell,

351
00:23:47,190 --> 00:23:50,750
the granules are all going to pile up right adjacent to the Golgi,

352
00:23:51,170 --> 00:23:55,390
and so when these pile up adjacent to the Golgi, we're going to see giant granules.

353
00:23:55,490 --> 00:23:58,570
They're going to appear as giant granules within the leukocyte,

354
00:23:59,050 --> 00:24:01,930
and so that's another important feature, and that's a histologic feature.

355
00:24:02,050 --> 00:24:03,390
Again, all of these are high yield.

356
00:24:03,390 --> 00:24:09,410
Another problem that's going to occur in these patients is that they're going to get defective primary hemostasis.

357
00:24:09,970 --> 00:24:11,590
Now, same sort of idea.

358
00:24:12,050 --> 00:24:14,870
Primary hemostasis is dependent on the platelets,

359
00:24:16,050 --> 00:24:19,870
and the platelets contain granules, for example, dense core granules,

360
00:24:20,150 --> 00:24:26,610
and if the granules are not properly distributed and cannot be properly functioning within the platelets,

361
00:24:26,910 --> 00:24:29,010
there'll be a defect in primary hemostasis.

362
00:24:29,830 --> 00:24:32,510
Okay, another finding is albinism, high yield,

363
00:24:32,510 --> 00:24:35,390
and this kind of also goes back to the similar principle.

364
00:24:35,770 --> 00:24:38,630
Let's go back and remind ourselves that the pigment of the skin,

365
00:24:38,790 --> 00:24:42,310
the pigmenting of the skin, actually occurs via the melanocytes.

366
00:24:42,790 --> 00:24:45,850
Now, what happens is the melanocytes, so if this is skin,

367
00:24:46,290 --> 00:24:50,590
and you've sort of got multiple keratinocytes that make up the skin,

368
00:24:50,770 --> 00:24:53,850
so we're just going to draw these here as keratinocytes.

369
00:24:54,310 --> 00:24:57,830
Within the keratinocytes, there is a rare melanocyte,

370
00:24:57,910 --> 00:24:59,730
which I've sort of drawn here, I'm filling it in.

371
00:24:59,730 --> 00:25:05,370
And this melanocyte produces the melanin, which is the pigment of the skin,

372
00:25:05,450 --> 00:25:07,370
for about 25 different keratinocytes.

373
00:25:07,710 --> 00:25:11,610
So one melanocyte produces the pigment for 25 different keratinocytes.

374
00:25:12,090 --> 00:25:16,390
And recall that the way by which the melanocyte sort of pigments the keratinocytes

375
00:25:16,390 --> 00:25:20,710
is to produce the pigment and then to hand it off to these different cells.

376
00:25:20,710 --> 00:25:25,750
And so imagine if the melanocytes are able to produce the pigment,

377
00:25:25,890 --> 00:25:30,430
but they're not able to hand it or pass it along the railroad system to the keratinocytes.

378
00:25:30,630 --> 00:25:34,550
If it can't be moved along the railroad system and passed to the keratinocytes,

379
00:25:35,010 --> 00:25:38,390
we're not going to get proper pigmentation of the skin,

380
00:25:38,730 --> 00:25:40,590
and that's going to result in albinism.

381
00:25:40,990 --> 00:25:43,210
And so that's a clinical feature of this disease as well.

382
00:25:43,630 --> 00:25:46,030
And the final clinical feature is peripheral neuropathy.

383
00:25:46,030 --> 00:25:49,430
And again, same thing, you know, I mean, I'm going to draw you a little stick figure

384
00:25:49,430 --> 00:25:52,410
because I don't have the ability, as you've probably already figured out,

385
00:25:52,450 --> 00:25:54,390
I don't have the ability to draw very well.

386
00:25:54,690 --> 00:25:58,630
But if we've got a nerve at the very periphery here in the body,

387
00:25:58,710 --> 00:26:01,090
let's pretend that that's the nerve of the toe.

388
00:26:01,450 --> 00:26:04,550
Remember that the cell body for the nerve of the toe is way up here,

389
00:26:04,790 --> 00:26:07,770
near the spinal cord.

390
00:26:08,330 --> 00:26:10,990
And so this actual nerve is, you know,

391
00:26:11,050 --> 00:26:13,990
it might be in some people up to two feet or sometimes even longer.

392
00:26:13,990 --> 00:26:17,110
Remember, imagine if the nucleus is up here

393
00:26:17,110 --> 00:26:19,470
and all of the key proteins are being produced up here,

394
00:26:19,750 --> 00:26:23,430
you're going to need the railroad system to be able to keep the bottom of the nerve

395
00:26:23,430 --> 00:26:26,750
or the very, very distal part of the nerve healthy and alive.

396
00:26:27,190 --> 00:26:30,910
And so if patients have a railroad defect or a protein trafficking defect,

397
00:26:31,270 --> 00:26:33,890
they can't really keep alive these peripheral nerves,

398
00:26:34,190 --> 00:26:36,710
and so they get something called a peripheral neuropathy.

399
00:26:36,850 --> 00:26:38,150
I've kind of simplified it a bit,

400
00:26:38,150 --> 00:26:41,650
but it's good enough for sort of general principles

401
00:26:41,650 --> 00:26:44,290
and creating global ideas within your mind.

402
00:26:44,850 --> 00:26:49,430
So, you know, these are the key features of a disease called Chediac-Higashi syndrome,

403
00:26:50,430 --> 00:26:52,410
and it's something very high-yield,

404
00:26:52,810 --> 00:26:54,490
something that we should be familiar with.

405
00:26:54,990 --> 00:26:58,610
So that was a derivative discussion that we had

406
00:26:58,610 --> 00:27:02,090
concerning the formation of phagocytosis

407
00:27:02,090 --> 00:27:03,990
and the formation of a phagolysisosome.

